## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React uses `Object.is` (shallow equality) to decide whether props changed. Primitives compare by value, but objects and functions compare by reference — a new reference means a new value, even if the contents are identical.

```jsx
// Re-renders Child on every Parent render — new object reference each time
<Child config={{ theme: 'dark' }} />
<Child onClick={() => handleClick()} />
```

Every render of `Parent` creates a fresh `{}` and a fresh `() => {}`, so `Child` always sees new props.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component, skips re-render if all props are shallowly equal.

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ config, onClick }) => { ... });
```

Use it when: a child is expensive to render and its props are stable or memoized. Don't use it unconditionally — the comparison itself has cost.

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a computed *value*.

```jsx
const config = useMemo(() => ({ theme: 'dark' }), []); // stable reference
```

Use it when: constructing an object/array passed as a prop, or a derived value that's expensive to compute.

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a *function* reference.

```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(id), [id]);
```

Use it when: passing a callback to a `React.memo` child or as a dependency of another hook. Pointless without `React.memo` on the receiver — the function is stable but the child still re-renders.

**Mental model:** `useCallback(fn, deps)` is just `useMemo(() => fn, deps)`.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Context triggers a re-render in **every consumer** when the value reference changes — not just components that use the specific field that changed.

```jsx
// Bad: new object on every render
<AuthContext.Provider value={{ user, logout }}>
```

Every render of the parent creates a new `{ user, logout }` object, blasting all consumers.

```jsx
// Better: stabilize the value
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, logout }), [user, logout]);
<AuthContext.Provider value={value}>
```

For large contexts, **split contexts** by update frequency: put stable values (static config) in one context, frequently-changing values (current user selection) in another. Consumers only subscribe to what they need.

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before — re-renders `ProductCard` on every `Page` render:**

```jsx
function Page({ productId }) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // New object reference every render
  const style = { border: '1px solid red', padding: 8 };

  // New function reference every render
  const handleAdd = () => addToCart(productId);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Clicked {count}</button>
      <ProductCard style={style} onAdd={handleAdd} />
    </>
  );
}

function ProductCard({ style, onAdd }) {
  console.log('ProductCard rendered'); // fires on every count change
  return <div style={style}><button onClick={onAdd}>Add</button></div>;
}
```

**After — `ProductCard` only re-renders when `productId` changes:**

```jsx
function Page({ productId }) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const style = useMemo(() => ({ border: '1px solid red', padding: 8 }), []);
  const handleAdd = useCallback(() => addToCart(productId), [productId]);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Clicked {count}</button>
      <ProductCard style={style} onAdd={handleAdd} />
    </>
  );
}

const ProductCard = React.memo(function ProductCard({ style, onAdd }) {
  console.log('ProductCard rendered'); // only fires when productId changes
  return <div style={style}><button onClick={onAdd}>Add</button></div>;
});
```

**The fix requires all three pieces together:** `useMemo` for the object, `useCallback` for the function, and `React.memo` on the child. Any one missing and the optimization collapses.

---

### Quick Decision Guide

| Situation | Tool |
|---|---|
| Expensive child with stable props | `React.memo` on child |
| Object/array passed as prop | `useMemo` |
| Callback passed to memoized child | `useCallback` |
| Context value object | `useMemo` on the value |
| Context with mixed update rates | Split into multiple contexts |

**Profile first** with React DevTools Profiler before adding memoization — premature optimization adds complexity for no gain.
